import
	../../html

intro-collections html[]()
	- section "Building immutable collections",
		- p quote
			Mutable collections can start out empty and be built up.
			This doesn't work for immutable collections.
			{br}
			Below are the ways to initialize immutable collections, starting with the simplest:
		- bullets
			- "Using {fn-new} or {fn-list-new}. See {intro-link new_}."
			- "Using {for_} or {for-star} loop. See {intro-link iteration}."
			- quote
				Using {fn-build}. This exposes a {builder} which is used to add elements one at a time.
				{br}
				There is no way to inspect the elements in a {builder} until it's finished.
			- quote
				Using a {mut-array}, then calling {fn-move-to} at the end. This is the most flexible approach.

		- p "To demonstrate each method:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log (1, 2, 3)::nat[]
				log
					:: nat[]
					- 1
					-- 2, 3
				log for::nat[] x in 0 .. 3
					x + 1
				log for*::nat[] x in 0 .. 3
					- x + 1
				log with::nat[] out in build
					out ~= 1
					out ~~= 2, 3
				log
					out nat mut[] = ()
					out ~= 1
					out ~~= 2, 3
					move-to::nat[] out

		- p "All of these methods can also be used to initialize mutable collections."

	- section "Arrays",
		- p quote
			The basic {array} operations are {fn-size} and {fn-subscript}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat[] = 1, 2, 3
				log size a
				log a[0]

		- p quote
			{std-function "keen/col/array", "~~"} concatenates two arrays.
			{std-function "keen/col/array", "~"} prepends or appends an element to an array.

	- section "Array views",
		- p quote
			An {array-view} is a slice of an original {array}.
			It has similar functionality to an array, but with different performance.
			{br}
			Using an {array-view} avoids copying the array elements,
			but holds onto the entire original array even if only viewing a small part.
			{br}
			It also adds a level of indirection.
			{br}
			You should usually use arrays and only use a view when there is a clear advantage.
		- p "There are two ways to get an {array-view}:"
		- bullets
			- "An {array} can implicitly convert to an {array-view}."
			- quote
				Slicing an array returns an {array-view}:
				{var-a}{lbracket}{num-2}{sp}{function ".."}{sp}{num-5}{rbracket}"

		- p quote
			Slicing can also index relative to the array size, such as
			{var-a}{lbracket}{num-1}{sp}{function ".."}{sp}{function "end"}{rbracket}
			which is equivalent to
			{var-a}{lbracket}{num-1}{sp}{function ".."}{sp}{var-a}{dot}{fn-size}{rbracket} .

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat[] = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
				b nat array-view = a
				log b
				c nat array-view = a[1 .. 3]
				log c
				d nat array-view = a[1 .. end - 1]
				log d

		- p "To slice an {array} and get another {array}, use {fn-to}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat[] = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
				log to::nat[] a[1 .. 3]

	- section "Collection conversion",
		- p quote
			Most collections define at least two {fn-to} functions:
			one converting to an array and one converting from an array.
			This makes it possible to convert between any two collections by going through an array.
		- p quote
			To convert to another collection while emptying the original collection,
			there is often a {fn-move-to} function.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat mut[] = 1, 2, 3
				b = a.move-to::nat[]
				log a, b

	- section "Mutable arrays",
		- p quote
			A mutable array is written {type "t mut[]"}, equivalent to {t}{sp}{mut-array} .

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat mut[] = ()
				a ~= 1
				log a

		- p "To prevent bugs, a {mut-array} can't be mutated during iteration."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat mut[] = 1, 2, 3
				for x in a
					# This will fail
					a ~= x
				log a

		- aside-small "You can still manually iterate the indices and mutate it during that."

	- section "Buffers",
		- p quote
			A {buffer} is like an {array} with mutable elements, but unlike a {mut-array}, it is fixed in size.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat buffer = for x in 0 .. 10 then x
				a[5] := 55
				log a

		- p quote
			{buffer}s aren't common, but are useful for low-level code.
			For example, {mut-array} is implemented using a {buffer}.
			{br}
			Low-level {buffer} use typically uses {std-function "keen/col/buffer", "uninitialized-buffer"},
			which is {unsafe}. (See {intro-link unsafe}.)

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat buffer = trusted uninitialized-buffer 10
				a[5] := 55
				# It's only safe to access elements that have been initialized
				log a[5]

		- p quote
			A {buffer-view} works like an {array-view}.
			{br}
			Writing to a {buffer-view} affects the original buffer.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat buffer = n-of 10, 0
				view = a[5 .. 10]
				view[1] := 6
				log a

	- section "Maps",
		- p quote
			A map is a collection of (key, value) pairs with unique keys
			that supports efficiently getting a value from the key.
			{br}
			The key must satisfy the {spec-key} spec, which means
			{fn-equal}, {fn-compare}, and {fn-hash} must be defined for it.

		- p quote
			A map type is written as {value-subscript-key},
			equivalent to {key-value}{sp}{type "map"}.

		- p quote
			Map lookup uses the same {fn-subscript} syntax as for arrays.
			Unlike for an array, it returns an option instead of throwing an exception on a missing key.
		- p "A map is constructed from a collection of tuples and iterates as tuples too."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a string[nat] =
					- 1, "one"
					- 2, "two"
				for x in a
					log x
				log "a[1]", a[1]
				log "a[3]", a[3]

		- p "If the map key is {symbol}, it can be created with {fn-named-new} syntax."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a string[symbol] =
					one: "uno"
					two: "dos"
				log a["two"]

	- section "Map iteration",
		- p quote
			Maps are designed for efficient lookups. To accomplish this, they internally use hashing.
			{br}
			Hashing depends on a random seed.
			This makes it unsafe to expose to safe code (which should not behave randomly).
		- p quote
			To keep it safe, map iteration sorts the map first.
			{br}
			If you will need to iterate a map frequently, consider using a {sorted-map}.
			This has the same functions as {map} but is always kept sorted, making iteration fast.
			The cost is that {sorted-map} is slower to look up keys than {map}.
		- p quote
			If you need both fast iteration and the fastest possible lookups,
			you could call {fn-map-unsorted} before iterating the map.
			This is {unsafe}. (That will be explained in {intro-link unsafe}.)

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat[symbol] = beta: 2, alpha: 1
				for x in a
					log x
				log "unsorted:"
				trusted for x in unsorted a
					log x

		- aside-small quote
			You should only use {fn-map-unsorted} iteration when you are certain iteration order doesn't matter.

	- section "Mutable maps",
		- p quote
			A mutable map is written as {type "value"}{sp}{mut_}{keyword "["}{type "key"}{keyword "]"},
			equivalent to {key-value}{sp}{col-type "mut-map"}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat mut[symbol] = ()
				a["alpha"] := 1
				a["beta"] := 2
				log a["alpha"]
				log remove a, "beta"
				log a["beta"]

		- p "Like with {map}, there is a {mut-sorted-map} that iterates faster at the expense of slower lookups."

	- section "Other collections",
		- p quote
			There are other collections in the standard library.
			Each has immutable and mutable versions.
			{br}
			There are also {shared_} collections, which will be explained in {intro-link parallel}.
		- table
			- thead
				- th "Name"
				- th "Use"
			- tbody
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "counter"}, {col-type "mut-counter"}"
					- td "Tracks how many of each value it contains."
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "deque"}, {col-type "mut-deque"}"
					- td "Fast to push or pop to/from either end."
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "priority-queue"}, {col-type "mut-priority-queue"}"
					- td "(key, value) pairs sorted by key, with fast removal of the least key."
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "set"}, {col-type "mut-set"}"
					- td "Stores only unique values, with fast test for inclusion."
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "sorted-set"}, {col-type "mut-sorted-set"}"
					- td "{col-type "set"} with slower lookups but fast iteration."
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "stack"}, {col-type "mut-stack"}"
					- td "Fast to push or pop to/from the right."
				- tr
					- td "{col-type "queue"}, {col-type "mut-queue"}"
					- td "Fast to push to the right or pop from the left."

-builder html-node()
	std-type "keen/col/util", "builder"

-fn-map-unsorted html-node()
	std-function "keen/col/map", "unsorted"

-sorted-map html-node()
	col-type "sorted-map"
-mut-sorted-map html-node()
	col-type "mut-sorted-map"
